# TEC Knowledge Base — Agent Rules (Cursor + any AI agent)

You are editing the TEC platform's constitutional knowledge base. These rules are
NON-NEGOTIABLE. Breaking them fails CI. Read before editing anything.

## 🔴 RULE 1 — The asset registry is AUTO-GENERATED. Never hand-edit it.

`architecture/asset-registry.yaml` is generated from the C-doc headers. If you edit
ANY `knowledge-base/C-*.md` file — even a single header value — you MUST regenerate
the registry and commit BOTH files together:

```bash
python3 scripts/build-asset-registry.py     # rebuild from the edited headers
git add knowledge-base/ architecture/asset-registry.yaml architecture/registry-integrity-report.md
```

- ❌ Editing `asset-registry.yaml` by hand → the **Registry Integrity Check** CI gate
  FAILS with: `asset-registry.yaml has manual edits that conflict with auto-generation`.
- ❌ Editing a C-doc header but NOT rebuilding the registry → same failure (the
  committed registry no longer matches the file). This is the single most common way
  these PRs fail — do not repeat it.

## 🔴 RULE 2 — Valid Truth-Framework header tokens

Every C-doc header declares three fields. Only these values are valid:

```
Truth State:        [Current State] | [Planned State] | [Future Vision] | [Speculation]
Governance State:   [Governance Approved] | [ADR Approved] | [Draft] | [Rejected]
Verification:       [Documentation Verified] | [Code Verified] | [Runtime Verified] | [Assumed]
```

- `[Unverified]` is **NOT** a valid Verification token. For `[Future Vision]` /
  `[Planned State]` docs the correct value is **`[Assumed]`**. (Note: some docs carry
  the header inline on one line, e.g. `> Truth State: … | … | Verification: [Assumed]` —
  fix those too; do not miss C-83.)
- Do NOT change the literal string `[Unverified]` where it appears in PROSE/tables that
  document the schema itself — only fix real header **values**.

## 🔴 RULE 3 — Before opening a PR, run the gates locally

```bash
python3 scripts/build-asset-registry.py
for g in evals/check-registry-integrity.sh evals/check-truth-framework.sh \
         evals/check-c57-index.sh evals/check-links.sh evals/check-vam-compliance.sh; do
  echo "== $g =="; bash "$g" || echo "FAILED: $g";
done
```
Open the PR only when Registry Integrity shows **0 errors**. There are 13 gates in
`evals/`; the registry + truth-framework + c57-index gates are the ones edits break.

## RULE 4 — Adding a new C-doc

Update `knowledge-base/C-57___MASTER_CONTENTS_INDEX.md` (index + the right TIER),
add the standard 3-field header, THEN rebuild the registry (Rule 1). New ADRs are
registered in `C-64`; large decisions get their own C-doc (the ADR-007→C-76 pattern).

## RULE 5 — Don't open duplicate PRs

Check open PRs first. If a PR already proposes your change, update it or comment —
do not open a fresh near-identical PR. (Eight duplicate `[Unverified]→[Assumed]` PRs
were opened and all failed the same way before this rule existed.)

## 🔴 RULE 6 — AI Constitutional Guard (canonical numbering)

**The golden chain — memorize it:**

```
PDF  →  Canonical      (the constitutional index is the source of truth)
 ↓
Files (knowledge-base/C-NN.md)  →  Canonical in-repo (mirror the PDF; never invent)
 ↓
Registry (asset-registry.yaml)  →  Generated       (never hand-write an ID)
 ↓
Sessions / memory / audits      →  Must Follow      (only cite IDs that exist)
```

Constitutional IDs (`C-NN`) are LAW. The `C-NN.md` files in `knowledge-base/` are the
**canonical** in-repo source of every ID; `architecture/asset-registry.yaml` (generated)
and `C-57` (index) MUST mirror them exactly. Therefore, no AI agent (or human) may:

- **invent** a `C-number` out of thin air;
- **renumber** an existing doc — once assigned, an ID is permanent (the C-124→C-130
  renumber only happened *pre-assignment*, before any file/registry/index existed);
- **reuse** a retired/existing number for different content — one ID ⟷ exactly one doc;
- **replace** one doc's ID with another's, or reference an ID that has no backing file.

**C-number assignment is human authority.** An AI agent MAY draft the *content* of a new
constitutional document, but:

- it MUST NOT self-assign the final `C-number`; it proposes the **next unused integer**
  (highest existing + 1; see `CLAUDE.md` range table) and flags it for human ratification —
  e.g. author the draft as `C-XX___PLACEHOLDER.md` (the `XX` keeps it out of the registry
  builder, which only matches `C-\d+`);
- it MUST NOT cite a not-yet-created `C-number` as if it were canonical in any session log,
  `memory/` entry, `audit/`, or PR description — cite an ID only after the backing file
  exists and is registered.

Once a number *is* ratified, the new ID MUST land in all three surfaces together: the file,
the C-57 index (Rule 4), and the regenerated registry (Rule 1).

**Two CI gates enforce this** (both fail the build on drift):

- `evals/check-canonical-numbering.sh` — files ⟺ registry ⟺ C-57 index must agree exactly
  (no duplicate, invented, renumbered, orphaned, or missing ID).
- `evals/check-session-canonical.sh` — every `C-NN` cited in `memory/` or `audits/` must
  resolve to a real canonical file (catches invented/renumbered IDs leaking into the
  session/audit narrative — the "Sessions → Must Follow" link of the chain).

## Authority + full guidance

`CLAUDE.md` (repo root) is the complete guide — read it. Authority hierarchy:
`C-00 → C-67 → ADRs (C-64) → Current State docs → Runtime Evidence → Code`.
